@page "/summary-shape"

<PageTitle>Shaping the summary</PageTitle>
<h1>Shaping the summary</h1>
<p>Four parameters on <code>FormidableSummary</code> (the error summary's component) decide what its list says: names or messages, one entry per field or per issue, and how many entries it shows. Submit the empty form once, then work the toggles: the problems on the form stay where they are, and only the list changes.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "SupportTicket.cs", "SummaryShape.razor", "SummaryShape.razor.cs" })">
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at the labels before typing anything.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> an asterisk beside every label but <strong>Impact</strong>'s.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> <strong>Impact</strong>'s only rule is a warning, and a warning never blocks a submit.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong> with every box empty.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> seven errors about six fields in the error summary, two on <strong>Ticket reference</strong>, and <strong>Impact</strong>'s warning listed separately under them.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> The error summary lists each severity in a band, a list of its own.</div></li>
            <li>Tick <strong>Name the field instead of showing the message</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the same seven errors and one warning now read as field names, with <strong>Ticket reference</strong> listed twice.</div></li>
            <li>Tick <strong>One entry per field instead of one per issue</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> six entries, with <strong>Ticket reference</strong> listed once; the warning band is unchanged.</div></li>
            <li>Tick <strong>Cap each band at three entries</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the error band stops after three entries and says nothing about the other three; the warning band keeps its one.</div></li>
            <li>Tick <strong>Reveal what the cap held back</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "3 not listed" under the third entry, with a disclosure triangle beside it.</div></li>
            <li>Click "3 not listed".<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the three names the cap held back: <strong>Product</strong>, <strong>Version</strong> and <strong>Steps to reproduce</strong>.</div></li>
            <li>Untick <strong>One entry per field instead of one per issue</strong>, leaving the cap on.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> three entries, two of them <strong>Ticket reference</strong>, and "4 not listed" under them.</div></li>
            <li>Untick <strong>Reveal what the cap held back</strong>, leaving the cap on.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the line goes, and the error band ends after three entries with nothing in their place.</div></li>
            <li>Fill in every box except <strong>Impact</strong> (<code>TKT-2031</code>, a valid email address, anything elsewhere) and click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "Submitted" in the status line, with <strong>Impact</strong>'s warning still listed.</div></li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Ticket reference</strong>: required; shaped like <code>TKT-0000</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Requester</strong>: required.</li>
            <li><strong>Requester email</strong>: required; once filled in, a valid email address.</li>
            <li><strong>Product</strong>: required.</li>
            <li><strong>Version</strong>: required.</li>
            <li><strong>Steps to reproduce</strong>: required.</li>
            <li><strong>Impact</strong>: a warning while empty, which never blocks a submit.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <HowItWorks>
        <p>A summary is a list, and a list has decisions in it: whether an entry names the field or quotes the rule, whether a field breaking two rules gets one entry or two, and what to do when there are more entries than room. Four parameters on <code>FormidableSummary</code> make them, and each toggle above the form sets one.</p>
        <p>None of the toggles is a wrong setting: every combination is one a real form could ship. What you are choosing between is a list of complaints and a list of names, a long list and a short one.</p>
        <p>Most sample pages leave all four unset and take the summary as it comes. The <a href="dialog-submit">Dialog-first submit</a> page sets all four at once, to fit a list into a dialog. The dialog page's toggles are a different kind: each has a setting that does not work, so you can watch the failure. This page is about the four parameters themselves.</p>
        <p><code>ItemTemplate</code> sets what an entry's button contains. Left unset (the first toggle off), an entry shows the rule's message. The template this page passes renders the field's name instead, from the entry's <code>DisplayName</code>. That is the rule's own name: the <code>WithName(...)</code> the validator gave it, or FluentValidation's name for the property.</p>
        <p>An error that arrives with no name, as a server's can, shows its property path instead. An issue about the whole form shows <code>ModelLevelDisplayName</code> (an option naming the form itself, "This form" by default).</p>
        <p><code>GroupByField</code> lists one entry per field instead of one per issue. It keeps each field's first issue and drops the rest, so <strong>Ticket reference</strong> is listed once rather than twice. It groups within a band and never across the summary: a field with an error and a warning is listed once under each, one field described two ways rather than one description repeated. The warning band, holding one entry, has nothing to collapse.</p>
        <p><strong>Ticket reference</strong> has two rules, and an empty box breaks both: one field, two complaints. Named by field without grouping, it appears twice, which reads as a mistake in the list rather than a mistake in the form; <code>GroupByField</code> lists it once.</p>
        <p><strong>Requester email</strong> has two rules too, but its address rule applies only once the box holds something. So an empty box breaks one, and the empty form shows seven errors about six fields.</p>
        <p><code>MaxItems</code> is the most entries a band lists, three here. It counts entries, and an entry is an issue by default or a field under <code>GroupByField</code>. So grouping and capping together list the first three fields with a problem, where capping alone lists the first three problems. The cap counts each band on its own: the error band loses entries while the warning band, holding one, never reaches it.</p>
        <p><code>OverflowTemplate</code> sets what a capped band shows after its last entry. It is handed the entries the cap held back, not just their number, so this page's line can name them. The page's template calls that list <code>held</code>; a line that only counted the entries would read <code>held.Count</code> and stop there. A count can tell you three were dropped. Only the entries can tell you <strong>Steps to reproduce</strong> was one of them.</p>
        <p>Leave <code>OverflowTemplate</code> unset with the cap on (the last toggle off), and a capped band shows nothing at all in place of what it dropped: no element, no sentence. The wording is the page's own, because a sentence has a language and a plural rule behind it that the component cannot pick.</p>
        <p><strong>Impact</strong>'s rule is a warning rather than an error when the box is empty. It fills a band of its own in the error summary, and it never blocks a submit. Its label carries no required mark for the same reason: the mark says a submit needs a value. Each mark is a <code>FormidableRequiredIndicator</code> (the component that draws it), which asks the validator, not the page, whether a submit needs the field.</p>
    </HowItWorks>
    <Demo>
        <div class="toggles">
            <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_nameTheField" /> Name the field instead of showing the message</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_groupByField" /> One entry per field instead of one per issue</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_capped" /> Cap each band at three entries</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_reveal" /> Reveal what the cap held back</label>
        </div>

        <FormidableForm Model="_ticket" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
            <div class="summary-slot">
                <FormidableSummary GroupByField="_groupByField"
                                   MaxItems="@(_capped ? 3 : (int?)null)"
                                   ItemTemplate="@(_nameTheField ? FieldName : null)"
                                   OverflowTemplate="@(_reveal ? HeldBack : null)" />
            </div>

            <div class="field"><label>Ticket reference <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _ticket.Reference" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_ticket.Reference" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _ticket.Reference" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Requester <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _ticket.Requester" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_ticket.Requester" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _ticket.Requester" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Requester email <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _ticket.RequesterEmail" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_ticket.RequesterEmail" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _ticket.RequesterEmail" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Product <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _ticket.Product" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_ticket.Product" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _ticket.Product" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Version <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _ticket.Version" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_ticket.Version" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _ticket.Version" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Steps to reproduce <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _ticket.Steps" /> <FormidableInputTextArea @bind-Value="_ticket.Steps" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _ticket.Steps" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Impact <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _ticket.Impact" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_ticket.Impact" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _ticket.Impact" /></div>

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="submit" class="primary">Submit</button>
            </div>
        </FormidableForm>

        <p role="status">@_status</p>
    </Demo>
</TeachingPanel>

@code {
    // Razor templates rather than <ItemTemplate>/<OverflowTemplate> child elements, because both
    // parameters have to be able to go back to null: with no ItemTemplate the component renders
    // the rule's message, and with no OverflowTemplate a capped band renders nothing at all in
    // place of what it dropped. A child-content element cannot be conditionally absent; a
    // parameter can.
    private RenderFragment<VisibleIssue> FieldName => entry => @<text>@EntryText(entry)</text>;

    // The entries, not their number. held.Count is the line's own count, and the list under it is
    // what a count cannot give: the names of the ones that did not fit. Each held-back entry is a
    // VisibleIssue, the same kind of entry ItemTemplate is handed for one that did fit, so one
    // method renders both.
    private RenderFragment<IReadOnlyList<VisibleIssue>> HeldBack => held =>
        @<details class="overflow-reveal">
            <summary>@held.Count not listed</summary>
            <ul>
                @foreach (var entry in held)
                {
                    <li>@EntryText(entry)</li>
                }
            </ul>
        </details>;
}
